WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.360
The human genome contains over 3
billion base pairs of genetic

2
00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:08.910
information. If it were stretched out
end to end, it would span approximately

3
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:15.760
6 feet. But each human cell that houses
all this information is about 0.0002

4
00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:20.850
inches across, approximately
360 thousand times smaller than the

5
00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.540
length of all of our genetic
information laid out side by side. So

6
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.980
how in the world are we able to fit
that massive amount of information into

7
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.480
a tiny cell? As always, nature came up
with a clever solution. Instead of all

8
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.510
that DNA being stuffed into cells as
long, sinuous strands which wouldn't

9
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:43.250
leave room for much else and would be
hard to untangle when the information

10
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:48.420
is needed, the information is coiled up
around a series of proteins into a

11
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.570
highly organized condensed structure
called chromatin.

12
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.440
As you'll see, it's similar to the
process of turning wool into a knitted

13
00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.960
sweater, where each piece provides
structure and organization to the next.

14
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:05.940
Let's explore this concept a bit more.
First, a small segment of the DNA

15
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.640
double helix is wrapped around a
collection of proteins called histones,

16
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:12.700
which forms a structure called a
nucleosome.

17
00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.450
Nucleusomes then fold on top of each
other into a single chromatin fiber.

18
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.870
From here, the chromatin fibers are
further coiled and intertwined into a

19
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:29.870
chromosome, which organizes our genetic
information in the form of genes, units

20
00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:35.620
of DNA that provide instructions to
make proteins. Each human cell has 23

21
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:41.330
pairs of chromosomes, or 46 total,
which contain approximately 20,000 to

22
00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:48.060
25,000 genes, all of which live inside
the cell's nucleus. From here, all that

23
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:52.660
neatly packaged information is
responsible for making sure each cell,

24
00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:57.960
and in turn our whole body, functions
properly. It turns out that this

25
00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.830
packaging trick doesn't just save space
inside a cell, but also makes sure DNA

26
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.680
is able to do its job properly. Think
of it like a coiled up elastic band;

27
00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:12.490
when it is compacted together, no
individual strands are accessible, but

28
00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:17.450
you can pull away particular pieces of
the coil band as needed. Similarly,

29
00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:21.080
when DNA is tightly wound around
histones, also called heterochromatin,

30
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:26.980
it isn't accessible to the proteins
required to read DNA, so those genes

31
00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:31.000
remain turned off. In response to
environmental changes, however,

32
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:35.610
histones are altered through a variety
of molecular processes which triggers

33
00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:39.580
the DNA to uncoil into a more relaxed
state, known as euchromatin.

34
00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.810
Euchromatin is accessible to the DNA
reading proteins, thus turning the

35
00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:50.280
necessary gene on. These dynamic
changes in chromatin density, a process

36
00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.890
called chromatin remodeling, are
important for the process of cell

37
00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:58.960
division— when cells copy all of their
genetic material and then segregate and

38
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.880
repackage it into the newly formed
daughter cell. In non-dividing cells,

39
00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:08.840
chromatin remodeling allows for tighter
control over when and where certain

40
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.970
genes are activated, which ensures
complex organisms, like humans, run

41
00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:18.590
like a well-oiled machine. Beyond
ensuring the right DNA is accessible at

42
00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:23.410
the right time, this packing structure
also prevents DNA from getting tangled,

43
00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:28.880
which reduces DNA damage. The structure
of chromatin is one of biology's many

44
00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:33.990
ingenious solutions to a mathematical
challenge and simultaneously solves

45
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.170
numerous cellular complexities. Number
one, it ensures vast amounts of genetic

46
00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:44.690
information can fit into each cell.
Number two, it enables complex

47
00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.350
organisms to diversify their
functionality. And number three, it

48
00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:55.130
helps prevent DNA damage. More levels
of biological intricacy, however, also

49
00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:59.800
means more opportunities for mistakes
to happen. Researchers are finding that

50
00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:04.450
genetic mutations affecting chromatin
structure are common in many types of

51
00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:09.330
diseases, especially cancer. While
scientists are still trying to uncover

52
00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:14.510
the precise roles these mutations play
in diseases, efforts to develop drugs

53
00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.959
that target chromatin machinery have
shown promise in early clinical studies,

54
00:04:19.339 --> 00:04:23.660
revealing their potential to provide a
new type of medicine for challenging

55
00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:24.650
health problems.

